.TH PASSWD 1
.SH NAME
passwd \- change user password
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B auth/passwd
[
.BI -u " user"
] [
.BI -s " signer"
] [
.I keyfile
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Passwd
changes the secret shared between the invoker
and the authentication server
.I signer
(default:
.BR $SIGNER ).
The
.I signer
must offer the
.IR keysrv (4)
service.
.PP
The secret is associated with a remote user name that need not be
the same as the name of the invoking user on the local system.
That remote user name is specified by a certificate signed by
.IR signer ,
and obtained from
.IR keyfile .
.I Keyfile
identifies a file containing a certificate (default:
.LR default ).
If
.I keyfile
is not an absolute pathname,
the file used will be
.BI /usr/ user /keyring/ keyfile.
.I User
by default is the invoking user's name (read from
.BR /dev/user ),
but the
.B -u
option can name another.
.PP
.I Passwd
connects to the
.IR signer ,
authenticating using the certificate in
.IR keyfile ,
and checks that the server it has reached authenticated as the signer of
that certificate
.RI ( keysrv (4)
runs with the signer's own key).
If it is anything else,
.I passwd
says what it is and stops before it asks for a secret:
a holder of some other certificate of the same signer would otherwise be sent
the proof of the old secret and then the new one.
.PP
.I Passwd
then prompts for the (remote) user's old secret, to double-check identity, then prompts for a new one, which must be confirmed.
The old secret is proved to the server by answering a challenge, never sent;
the new one is sent as its SHA-256, which is what the server stores and
.IR login (6)
uses, sealed under a key made from the old secret
(see
.IR keysrv (4)).
Secrets are typed with the console's echo off and edited as they are read
(backspace, control-U; see
.B readsecret
in
.IR security-login (2)),
kept as bytes and zeroed once used.
An empty reply to the first prompt, or one that cannot be read, changes nothing
and is an error; an empty reply to the second leaves the secret as it is.
A wrong old secret is asked for again; the server counts each one against
the name, with failed logins, and refuses the name for a minute after ten.
.PP
Secrets must be at least eight characters long.
Try to make them hard to guess.
.SH FILES
.TF /mnt/keysrv
.TP
.B /dev/user
current user name
.TP
.B /mnt/keysrv
local mount point for connection to
remote
.IR keysrv (4)
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/auth/passwd.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR keyfs (4),
.IR keysrv (4),
.IR login (6),
.IR changelogin (8),
.IR logind (8)
